window.ICSE_PRACTICE=window.ICSE_PRACTICE||{};window.ICSE_PRACTICE["english2"]={"bank":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?","answer":"Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-002","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger.","His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why is Brutus angry about Lucius Pella’s case?","answer":"Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation. He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar. Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-011","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation.","He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar.","Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"How does Strato assist Brutus at the end?","answer":"After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it. This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-023","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it.","This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How do the photographer’s comments on the narrator’s face establish his attitude?","answer":"He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material. His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-026","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material.","His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?","answer":"The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-027","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned.","Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Explain the irony in the photographer’s confidence about retouching.","answer":"He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements. For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness. Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-028","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements.","For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness.","Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the finished photograph fail even if its features are considered attractive?","answer":"Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion. It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-030","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion.","It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does the narrator’s reaction at the end expose the limits of professional expertise?","answer":"He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him. The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-031","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him.","The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.","answer":"Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-033","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him.","Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?","answer":"He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-034","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it.","The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?","answer":"She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-035","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin.","Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does using the stairs fail to solve Martin’s problem?","answer":"The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him. Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-036","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him.","Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Explain how repeated meetings with the woman affect the reader.","answer":"Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern. Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-038","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern.","Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"What effect is produced by the woman’s final greeting to Martin?","answer":"Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear. The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-039","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear.","The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What is Nana’s objection to Adjoa’s legs?","answer":"She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-041","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why does Adjoa find adult conversation difficult to understand?","answer":"Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her. Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-042","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her.","Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does Adjoa’s running challenge Nana’s judgement?","answer":"The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance. Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-043","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance.","Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?","answer":"It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-044","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance.","The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What does Nana’s response to Adjoa’s achievement reveal?","answer":"Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism. She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-045","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism.","She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Explain the significance of the title in relation to Adjoa.","answer":"It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume. The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-047","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume.","The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the absence of other pedestrians matter?","answer":"It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional. It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-049","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional.","It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the police car regard Mead’s profession as suspicious or incomprehensible?","answer":"His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens. The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-051","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens.","The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How does the automated police car represent social authority?","answer":"Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories. Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-052","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories.","Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?","answer":"Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-053","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens.","Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"What is ironic about the institution to which Mead is taken?","answer":"Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction. The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-054","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction.","The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the contrast between Mead’s home and the others matter near the end?","answer":"His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life. He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-055","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life.","He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is Franz initially reluctant to go to school?","answer":"He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand. The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-057","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand.","The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What unusual features of the classroom signal a change?","answer":"The usual noise is absent, M. Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back. These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-058","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["The usual noise is absent, M.","Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back.","These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What does Franz discover about the official order?","answer":"Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine. The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M. Hamel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-059","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine.","The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M.","Hamel."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does Franz’s attitude towards his books change?","answer":"Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language. Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-060","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language.","Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?","answer":"Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-063","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Emotion overwhelms him.","He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How do the poem’s ghosts differ from frightening conventional ghosts?","answer":"They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions. The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-065","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions.","The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?","answer":"Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-066","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before.","Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?","answer":"The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-067","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place.","Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?","answer":"It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-068","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives.","Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the poem connect the material and spiritual worlds?","answer":"It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate. Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-069","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate.","Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why does the calm tone matter to the treatment of death?","answer":"It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural. The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-070","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural.","The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why is the setting at a royal court important?","answer":"The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-073","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The spectators and king make the act a public display.","The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How are the lions presented before the glove is dropped?","answer":"Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger. This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-074","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger.","This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why does the lover retrieve the glove but reject the lady?","answer":"Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him. He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-076","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him.","He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the king’s response contribute to the ending?","answer":"It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity. Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-077","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity.","Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the opening describe reactions in the natural world?","answer":"The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss. The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-082","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss.","The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the poem represent the immediate effect of bereavement on perception?","answer":"The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception. Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur. This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities. The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-083","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception.","Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur.","This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities.","The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What role does memory play after the death of a great person?","answer":"Memory recalls the person’s presence and the opportunities for appreciation that were missed. It makes absence painful while preserving the influence of the life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-084","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Memory recalls the person’s presence and the opportunities for appreciation that were missed.","It makes absence painful while preserving the influence of the life."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?","answer":"They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-087","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives.","Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the speck first easy to dismiss?","answer":"Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page. Close observation challenges that first judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-089","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page.","Close observation challenges that first judgement."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Which aspect of the speck’s behaviour suggests intelligence to the speaker?","answer":"Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion. The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-090","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion.","The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?","answer":"He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-091","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["He could destroy the creature easily while writing.","Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the title an apparent contradiction?","answer":"A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-092","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance.","The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does humour affect the speaker’s reflection?","answer":"The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful. It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-093","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful.","It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"What does the decision to spare the speck reveal?","answer":"It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power. The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-095","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power.","The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What makes the singer’s confidence comic?","answer":"He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-097","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress.","The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?","answer":"The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-098","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area.","Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How does the poem treat appeals to stop the singing?","answer":"Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer. Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-100","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer.","Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why is the goat’s intervention an effective ending?","answer":"An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot. Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-101","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot.","Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What is ironic about the title?","answer":"Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony. Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-102","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony.","Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}],"papers":[{"id":"english2-paper-01","number":1,"title":"English Literature — Paper 01","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a detective’s murder investigation","a war at sea","continuity of past lives in familiar places","the invention of a machine"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes","a race already won","traditional expectations of a woman’s body"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["participles","fractions","Roman history","musical scales"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a mechanical clock","a printed correction","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a comic musical performance","only a change in weather","influential lives whose loss affects others","a forestry instruction manual"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["a private taxi","automated","drawn by horses","driven by Mead"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Titinius","Metellus Cimber","Strato","Caesar"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","painting portraits","running","writing a newspaper"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage","a forest expedition"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["French","German","all subjects","Latin everywhere"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators","fear preventing any action","self-respect over a vain demand"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Lucius","Pindarus","Cinna the poet","Octavius"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["ownership of a camera","a missing letter","the price of a train ticket","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a photographer","a royal judge","a goat","Martin’s father"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Calpurnia","Pindarus","Portia","Antony"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["driving a racing car","walking at night","robbing a bank","repairing a television"],"correct":1}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?","answer":"Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-002","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger.","His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the finished photograph fail even if its features are considered attractive?","answer":"Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion. It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-030","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion.","It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?","answer":"The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-027","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned.","Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What is Nana’s objection to Adjoa’s legs?","answer":"She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-041","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?","answer":"It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-044","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance.","The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Explain the significance of the title in relation to Adjoa.","answer":"It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume. The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-047","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume.","The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?","answer":"Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-066","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before.","Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the poem connect the material and spiritual worlds?","answer":"It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate. Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-069","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate.","Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?","answer":"The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-067","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place.","Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?","answer":"It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-068","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives.","Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the opening describe reactions in the natural world?","answer":"The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss. The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-082","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss.","The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-02","number":2,"title":"English Literature — Paper 02","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Philippi","Alexandria","Athens","Sardinia"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a chemical formula","a mechanical clock","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Calpurnia","Pindarus","Antony"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["bribery","a marriage proposal","a lost crown","a forged will"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a detective’s murder investigation","a war at sea"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["self-respect over a vain demand","hatred of all spectators","fear preventing any action","ignorance of danger"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","a missing letter","ownership of a camera","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","writing a newspaper","running","painting portraits"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["a private taxi","automated","driven by Mead","drawn by horses"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Caesar","Strato","Metellus Cimber","Titinius"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","vote for a new king","test of her lover’s devotion","lesson in medicine"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["French","German","all subjects","Latin everywhere"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["musical scales","Roman history","fractions","participles"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a forest expedition"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["Martin’s father","a photographer","a goat","a royal judge"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a comic musical performance","a forestry instruction manual","influential lives whose loss affects others","only a change in weather"],"correct":2}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why is Brutus angry about Lucius Pella’s case?","answer":"Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation. He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar. Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-011","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation.","He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar.","Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?","answer":"She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-035","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin.","Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does using the stairs fail to solve Martin’s problem?","answer":"The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him. Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-036","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him.","Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.","answer":"Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-033","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him.","Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the absence of other pedestrians matter?","answer":"It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional. It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-049","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional.","It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the police car regard Mead’s profession as suspicious or incomprehensible?","answer":"His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens. The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-051","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens.","The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the contrast between Mead’s home and the others matter near the end?","answer":"His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life. He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-055","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life.","He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the king’s response contribute to the ending?","answer":"It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity. Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-077","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity.","Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does humour affect the speaker’s reflection?","answer":"The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful. It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-093","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful.","It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Which aspect of the speck’s behaviour suggests intelligence to the speaker?","answer":"Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion. The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-090","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion.","The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"What does the decision to spare the speck reveal?","answer":"It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power. The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-095","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power.","The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-03","number":3,"title":"English Literature — Paper 03","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","vote for a new king","lesson in medicine","test of her lover’s devotion"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["French","German","Latin everywhere","all subjects"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Portia","Pindarus","Brutus","Strato"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","a comic musical performance","a forestry instruction manual","only a change in weather"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["robbing a bank","walking at night","repairing a television","driving a racing car"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Lucius","Octavius","Cinna the poet","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","a war at sea","a detective’s murder investigation","continuity of past lives in familiar places"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["hatred of all spectators","self-respect over a vain demand","fear preventing any action","ignorance of danger"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["participles","musical scales","Roman history","fractions"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","writing a newspaper","running","painting portraits"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Alexandria","Sardinia","Philippi","Athens"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Calpurnia","Antony","Pindarus"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["a missing letter","likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["automated","a private taxi","drawn by horses","driven by Mead"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a royal judge","a goat","a photographer","Martin’s father"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a lost crown","bribery","a forged will","a marriage proposal"],"correct":1}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Explain the significance of the title in relation to Adjoa.","answer":"It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume. The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-047","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume.","The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why does Adjoa find adult conversation difficult to understand?","answer":"Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her. Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-042","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her.","Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does Adjoa’s running challenge Nana’s judgement?","answer":"The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance. Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-043","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance.","Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?","answer":"Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-063","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Emotion overwhelms him.","He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What unusual features of the classroom signal a change?","answer":"The usual noise is absent, M. Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back. These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-058","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["The usual noise is absent, M.","Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back.","These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the poem represent the immediate effect of bereavement on perception?","answer":"The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception. Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur. This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities. The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-083","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception.","Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur.","This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities.","The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?","answer":"They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-087","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives.","Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How does the poem treat appeals to stop the singing?","answer":"Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer. Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-100","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer.","Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?","answer":"The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-098","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area.","Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What makes the singer’s confidence comic?","answer":"He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-097","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress.","The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-04","number":4,"title":"English Literature — Paper 04","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Caesar","Metellus Cimber","Strato","Titinius"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Sardinia","Alexandria","Athens","Philippi"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","lesson in medicine","test of her lover’s devotion","vote for a new king"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["a missing letter","ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Antony","Calpurnia","Pindarus","Portia"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a forest expedition","a school election","a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["writing a newspaper","running","painting portraits","playing the violin"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["bribery","a forged will","a lost crown","a marriage proposal"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["Latin everywhere","French","German","all subjects"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["Martin’s father","a goat","a photographer","a royal judge"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["traditional expectations of a woman’s body","the cost of shoes","a race already won","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["repairing a television","driving a racing car","robbing a bank","walking at night"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["self-respect over a vain demand","ignorance of danger","fear preventing any action","hatred of all spectators"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","a forestry instruction manual","only a change in weather","a comic musical performance"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Pindarus","Portia","Brutus","Strato"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a war at sea","the invention of a machine","a detective’s murder investigation","continuity of past lives in familiar places"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the police car regard Mead’s profession as suspicious or incomprehensible?","answer":"His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens. The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-051","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens.","The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How does the automated police car represent social authority?","answer":"Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories. Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-052","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories.","Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"What is ironic about the institution to which Mead is taken?","answer":"Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction. The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-054","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction.","The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Explain the irony in the photographer’s confidence about retouching.","answer":"He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements. For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness. Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-028","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements.","For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness.","Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does the narrator’s reaction at the end expose the limits of professional expertise?","answer":"He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him. The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-031","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him.","The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does humour affect the speaker’s reflection?","answer":"The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful. It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-093","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful.","It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?","answer":"He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-091","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["He could destroy the creature easily while writing.","Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the speck first easy to dismiss?","answer":"Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page. Close observation challenges that first judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-089","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page.","Close observation challenges that first judgement."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the title an apparent contradiction?","answer":"A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-092","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance.","The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?","answer":"Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-066","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before.","Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?","answer":"The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-067","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place.","Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How do the poem’s ghosts differ from frightening conventional ghosts?","answer":"They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions. The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-065","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions.","The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-05","number":5,"title":"English Literature — Paper 05","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["walking at night","driving a racing car","robbing a bank","repairing a television"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Pindarus","Calpurnia","Antony","Portia"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a marriage proposal","a lost crown","bribery","a forged will"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["Latin everywhere","German","French","all subjects"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Sardinia","Athens","Philippi","Alexandria"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["Roman history","participles","fractions","musical scales"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","painting portraits","running","writing a newspaper"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera","a missing letter"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a mechanical clock","a printed correction","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["continuity of past lives in familiar places","the invention of a machine","a war at sea","a detective’s murder investigation"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a race already won","a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","a forestry instruction manual","a comic musical performance","only a change in weather"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage","a school election","a forest expedition"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["drawn by horses","a private taxi","driven by Mead","automated"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Lucius","Octavius","Cinna the poet","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","vote for a new king","lesson in medicine","test of her lover’s devotion"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What does Franz discover about the official order?","answer":"Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine. The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M. Hamel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-059","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine.","The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M.","Hamel."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does Franz’s attitude towards his books change?","answer":"Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language. Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-060","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language.","Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?","answer":"Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-063","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Emotion overwhelms him.","He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What unusual features of the classroom signal a change?","answer":"The usual noise is absent, M. Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back. These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-058","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["The usual noise is absent, M.","Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back.","These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.","answer":"Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-033","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him.","Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?","answer":"She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-035","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin.","Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?","answer":"He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-034","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it.","The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How does the poem treat appeals to stop the singing?","answer":"Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer. Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-100","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer.","Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What is ironic about the title?","answer":"Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony. Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-102","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony.","Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?","answer":"The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-098","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area.","Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why is the setting at a royal court important?","answer":"The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-073","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The spectators and king make the act a public display.","The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-06","number":6,"title":"English Literature — Paper 06","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["only a change in weather","a comic musical performance","a forestry instruction manual","influential lives whose loss affects others"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","test of her lover’s devotion","lesson in medicine","vote for a new king"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["Latin everywhere","German","all subjects","French"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a race already won","a medical examination described in detail","traditional expectations of a woman’s body","the cost of shoes"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Pindarus","Strato","Brutus","Portia"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a detective’s murder investigation","the invention of a machine","a war at sea","continuity of past lives in familiar places"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["a missing letter","likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Metellus Cimber","Titinius","Caesar","Strato"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Pindarus","Cinna the poet","Lucius","Octavius"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage","a forest expedition","a school election"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["participles","musical scales","fractions","Roman history"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["repairing a television","walking at night","robbing a bank","driving a racing car"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Antony","Portia","Pindarus","Calpurnia"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a lost crown","bribery","a forged will","a marriage proposal"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Athens","Sardinia","Alexandria","Philippi"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["a private taxi","driven by Mead","drawn by horses","automated"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How do the photographer’s comments on the narrator’s face establish his attitude?","answer":"He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material. His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-026","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material.","His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?","answer":"The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-027","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned.","Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the finished photograph fail even if its features are considered attractive?","answer":"Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion. It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-030","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion.","It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?","answer":"It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-044","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance.","The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What does Nana’s response to Adjoa’s achievement reveal?","answer":"Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism. She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-045","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism.","She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does Adjoa’s running challenge Nana’s judgement?","answer":"The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance. Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-043","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance.","Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?","answer":"The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-067","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place.","Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?","answer":"Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-066","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before.","Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why does the calm tone matter to the treatment of death?","answer":"It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural. The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-070","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural.","The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the poem connect the material and spiritual worlds?","answer":"It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate. Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-069","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate.","Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the opening describe reactions in the natural world?","answer":"The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss. The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-082","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss.","The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the poem represent the immediate effect of bereavement on perception?","answer":"The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception. Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur. This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities. The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-083","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception.","Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur.","This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities.","The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-07","number":7,"title":"English Literature — Paper 07","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a royal judge","a photographer","a goat","Martin’s father"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Athens","Philippi","Alexandria","Sardinia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","only a change in weather","a comic musical performance","a forestry instruction manual"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["French","German","Latin everywhere","all subjects"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a detective’s murder investigation","a war at sea"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Pindarus","Brutus","Strato","Portia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a mechanical clock","apparently purposeful intelligence","a chemical formula"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["vote for a new king","lesson in medicine","proof of the king’s poverty","test of her lover’s devotion"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a forest expedition","a school election","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Metellus Cimber","Strato","Caesar","Titinius"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["repairing a television","robbing a bank","walking at night","driving a racing car"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","a missing letter","ownership of a camera","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["driven by Mead","drawn by horses","automated","a private taxi"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","running","writing a newspaper","painting portraits"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["ignorance of danger","fear preventing any action","hatred of all spectators","self-respect over a vain demand"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["the cost of shoes","a race already won","traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":2}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?","answer":"Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-002","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger.","His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"How does Strato assist Brutus at the end?","answer":"After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it. This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-023","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it.","This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?","answer":"He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-034","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it.","The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Explain how repeated meetings with the woman affect the reader.","answer":"Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern. Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-038","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern.","Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"What effect is produced by the woman’s final greeting to Martin?","answer":"Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear. The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-039","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear.","The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?","answer":"Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-053","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens.","Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How does the automated police car represent social authority?","answer":"Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories. Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-052","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories.","Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the police car regard Mead’s profession as suspicious or incomprehensible?","answer":"His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens. The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-051","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens.","The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How are the lions presented before the glove is dropped?","answer":"Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger. This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-074","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger.","This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why does the lover retrieve the glove but reject the lady?","answer":"Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him. He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-076","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him.","He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why is the setting at a royal court important?","answer":"The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-073","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The spectators and king make the act a public display.","The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does humour affect the speaker’s reflection?","answer":"The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful. It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-093","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful.","It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the title an apparent contradiction?","answer":"A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-092","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance.","The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?","answer":"He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-091","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["He could destroy the creature easily while writing.","Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-08","number":8,"title":"English Literature — Paper 08","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a lost crown","bribery","a forged will","a marriage proposal"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["vote for a new king","test of her lover’s devotion","proof of the king’s poverty","lesson in medicine"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a chemical formula","a printed correction","a mechanical clock","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a medical examination described in detail","traditional expectations of a woman’s body","the cost of shoes","a race already won"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["robbing a bank","walking at night","repairing a television","driving a racing car"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a forestry instruction manual","a comic musical performance","only a change in weather","influential lives whose loss affects others"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket","likeness versus imposed improvement","a missing letter"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Cinna the poet","Octavius","Lucius","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Antony","Portia","Calpurnia","Pindarus"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a forest expedition"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","a war at sea","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a detective’s murder investigation"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["drawn by horses","a private taxi","driven by Mead","automated"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","painting portraits","writing a newspaper","running"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Caesar","Titinius","Metellus Cimber","Strato"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a royal judge","a goat","Martin’s father","a photographer"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["self-respect over a vain demand","ignorance of danger","fear preventing any action","hatred of all spectators"],"correct":0}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?","answer":"Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-002","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger.","His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?","answer":"It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-044","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance.","The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does Adjoa’s running challenge Nana’s judgement?","answer":"The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance. Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-043","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance.","Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What does Nana’s response to Adjoa’s achievement reveal?","answer":"Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism. She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-045","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism.","She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?","answer":"Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-063","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Emotion overwhelms him.","He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What unusual features of the classroom signal a change?","answer":"The usual noise is absent, M. Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back. These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-058","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["The usual noise is absent, M.","Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back.","These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?","answer":"They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-087","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives.","Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What role does memory play after the death of a great person?","answer":"Memory recalls the person’s presence and the opportunities for appreciation that were missed. It makes absence painful while preserving the influence of the life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-084","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Memory recalls the person’s presence and the opportunities for appreciation that were missed.","It makes absence painful while preserving the influence of the life."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the opening describe reactions in the natural world?","answer":"The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss. The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-082","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss.","The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What is ironic about the title?","answer":"Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony. Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-102","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony.","Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What makes the singer’s confidence comic?","answer":"He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-097","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress.","The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-09","number":9,"title":"English Literature — Paper 09","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["robbing a bank","driving a racing car","repairing a television","walking at night"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Brutus","Strato","Portia","Pindarus"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Pindarus","Portia","Calpurnia","Antony"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["painting portraits","playing the violin","running","writing a newspaper"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["French","all subjects","Latin everywhere","German"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["apparently purposeful intelligence","a printed correction","a chemical formula","a mechanical clock"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","a missing letter","likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a detective’s murder investigation","the invention of a machine","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a war at sea"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Strato","Caesar","Metellus Cimber","Titinius"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["musical scales","participles","Roman history","fractions"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a forestry instruction manual","influential lives whose loss affects others","only a change in weather","a comic musical performance"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Octavius","Pindarus","Lucius","Cinna the poet"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a forest expedition","a school election"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["self-respect over a vain demand","fear preventing any action","ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a lost crown","a forged will","bribery","a marriage proposal"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a photographer","a royal judge","a goat","Martin’s father"],"correct":2}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?","answer":"Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-053","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens.","Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the police car regard Mead’s profession as suspicious or incomprehensible?","answer":"His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens. The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-051","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His writing has little recognised place in a society dominated by screens.","The machine treats deviation from the norm as a problem rather than valuing independent work."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"What is ironic about the institution to which Mead is taken?","answer":"Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction. The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-054","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction.","The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Explain the irony in the photographer’s confidence about retouching.","answer":"He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements. For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness. Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-028","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements.","For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness.","Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How do the photographer’s comments on the narrator’s face establish his attitude?","answer":"He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material. His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-026","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material.","His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?","answer":"The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-027","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned.","Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?","answer":"He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-091","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["He could destroy the creature easily while writing.","Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the speck first easy to dismiss?","answer":"Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page. Close observation challenges that first judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-089","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page.","Close observation challenges that first judgement."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Which aspect of the speck’s behaviour suggests intelligence to the speaker?","answer":"Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion. The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-090","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion.","The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why does the calm tone matter to the treatment of death?","answer":"It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural. The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-070","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural.","The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?","answer":"Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-066","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before.","Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?","answer":"It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-068","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives.","Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-10","number":10,"title":"English Literature — Paper 10","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence","a mechanical clock","a printed correction"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Octavius","Cinna the poet","Lucius","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a forest expedition"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a marriage proposal","a forged will","bribery","a lost crown"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket","a missing letter"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["automated","a private taxi","drawn by horses","driven by Mead"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["participles","Roman history","fractions","musical scales"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a race already won","the cost of shoes","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["Martin’s father","a goat","a royal judge","a photographer"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Strato","Brutus","Pindarus","Portia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["running","playing the violin","painting portraits","writing a newspaper"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Athens","Philippi","Alexandria","Sardinia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["robbing a bank","driving a racing car","walking at night","repairing a television"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Pindarus","Antony","Calpurnia"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Caesar","Titinius","Strato","Metellus Cimber"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a comic musical performance","influential lives whose loss affects others","only a change in weather","a forestry instruction manual"],"correct":1}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does Franz’s attitude towards his books change?","answer":"Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language. Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-060","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language.","Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What does Franz discover about the official order?","answer":"Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine. The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M. Hamel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-059","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine.","The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M.","Hamel."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is Franz initially reluctant to go to school?","answer":"He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand. The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-057","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand.","The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.","answer":"Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-033","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him.","Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?","answer":"She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-035","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin.","Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"What effect is produced by the woman’s final greeting to Martin?","answer":"Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear. The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-039","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear.","The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does using the stairs fail to solve Martin’s problem?","answer":"The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him. Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-036","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him.","Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?","answer":"The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-098","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area.","Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why is the goat’s intervention an effective ending?","answer":"An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot. Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-101","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot.","Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What makes the singer’s confidence comic?","answer":"He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-097","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress.","The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How are the lions presented before the glove is dropped?","answer":"Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger. This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-074","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger.","This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why is the setting at a royal court important?","answer":"The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-073","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The spectators and king make the act a public display.","The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-11","number":11,"title":"English Literature — Paper 11","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["bribery","a marriage proposal","a forged will","a lost crown"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a royal judge","a photographer","a goat","Martin’s father"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Metellus Cimber","Titinius","Strato","Caesar"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["robbing a bank","repairing a television","walking at night","driving a racing car"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a forestry instruction manual","a comic musical performance","only a change in weather","influential lives whose loss affects others"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","a missing letter","ownership of a camera","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["vote for a new king","proof of the king’s poverty","test of her lover’s devotion","lesson in medicine"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Antony","Calpurnia","Portia","Pindarus"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Athens","Philippi","Alexandria","Sardinia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a school election","a forest expedition","a sea voyage"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["automated","a private taxi","drawn by horses","driven by Mead"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["running","playing the violin","painting portraits","writing a newspaper"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a race already won","a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes","traditional expectations of a woman’s body"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["fear preventing any action","ignorance of danger","self-respect over a vain demand","hatred of all spectators"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["musical scales","fractions","participles","Roman history"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a detective’s murder investigation","a war at sea","continuity of past lives in familiar places","the invention of a machine"],"correct":2}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the finished photograph fail even if its features are considered attractive?","answer":"Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion. It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-030","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion.","It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Explain the irony in the photographer’s confidence about retouching.","answer":"He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements. For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness. Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-028","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements.","For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness.","Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why does Adjoa find adult conversation difficult to understand?","answer":"Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her. Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-042","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her.","Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?","answer":"It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-044","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance.","The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What is Nana’s objection to Adjoa’s legs?","answer":"She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-041","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How do the poem’s ghosts differ from frightening conventional ghosts?","answer":"They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions. The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-065","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions.","The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why does the calm tone matter to the treatment of death?","answer":"It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural. The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-070","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It makes coexistence with the past contemplative and natural.","The poem offers continuity rather than a sensational supernatural threat."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?","answer":"It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-068","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives.","Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the opening describe reactions in the natural world?","answer":"The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss. The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-082","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The disturbed animals and landscape give physical scale to loss.","The analogy prepares readers for the emotional and intellectual disruption caused by a great soul’s death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?","answer":"They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-087","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives.","Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-12","number":12,"title":"English Literature — Paper 12","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["bribery","a forged will","a marriage proposal","a lost crown"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a war at sea","a detective’s murder investigation"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a race already won","a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a mechanical clock","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Lucius","Cinna the poet","Pindarus","Octavius"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a royal judge","a goat","a photographer","Martin’s father"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Alexandria","Sardinia","Philippi","Athens"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","test of her lover’s devotion","vote for a new king","lesson in medicine"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera","a missing letter"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Portia","Brutus","Pindarus","Strato"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["German","Latin everywhere","French","all subjects"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["only a change in weather","a forestry instruction manual","influential lives whose loss affects others","a comic musical performance"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["a private taxi","driven by Mead","drawn by horses","automated"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a forest expedition","a school election","a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["driving a racing car","repairing a television","robbing a bank","walking at night"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["running","playing the violin","painting portraits","writing a newspaper"],"correct":0}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?","answer":"She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-035","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin.","Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does using the stairs fail to solve Martin’s problem?","answer":"The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him. Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-036","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him.","Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"What effect is produced by the woman’s final greeting to Martin?","answer":"Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear. The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-039","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear.","The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?","answer":"He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-034","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it.","The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How does the automated police car represent social authority?","answer":"Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories. Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-052","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories.","Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the absence of other pedestrians matter?","answer":"It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional. It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-049","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional.","It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the contrast between Mead’s home and the others matter near the end?","answer":"His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life. He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-055","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life.","He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the king’s response contribute to the ending?","answer":"It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity. Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-077","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity.","Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How are the lions presented before the glove is dropped?","answer":"Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger. This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-074","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger.","This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the speck first easy to dismiss?","answer":"Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page. Close observation challenges that first judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-089","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page.","Close observation challenges that first judgement."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?","answer":"He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-091","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["He could destroy the creature easily while writing.","Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"What does the decision to spare the speck reveal?","answer":"It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power. The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-095","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power.","The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-13","number":13,"title":"English Literature — Paper 13","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["fear preventing any action","ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators","self-respect over a vain demand"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a lost crown","a forged will","a marriage proposal","bribery"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["playing the violin","painting portraits","running","writing a newspaper"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Octavius","Lucius","Cinna the poet","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a forest expedition","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence","a mechanical clock"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["walking at night","repairing a television","robbing a bank","driving a racing car"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Brutus","Pindarus","Strato","Portia"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Philippi","Sardinia","Athens","Alexandria"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Caesar","Titinius","Strato","Metellus Cimber"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","a war at sea","a detective’s murder investigation","continuity of past lives in familiar places"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["a royal judge","a goat","a photographer","Martin’s father"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["participles","musical scales","fractions","Roman history"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","a missing letter","likeness versus imposed improvement","ownership of a camera"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["a private taxi","driven by Mead","drawn by horses","automated"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes","a race already won","traditional expectations of a woman’s body"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"How does Strato assist Brutus at the end?","answer":"After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it. This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-023","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it.","This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What is Nana’s objection to Adjoa’s legs?","answer":"She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-041","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What does Nana’s response to Adjoa’s achievement reveal?","answer":"Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism. She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-045","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism.","She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Explain the significance of the title in relation to Adjoa.","answer":"It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume. The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-047","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume.","The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is Franz initially reluctant to go to school?","answer":"He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand. The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-057","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand.","The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?","answer":"Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-063","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Emotion overwhelms him.","He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does Franz’s attitude towards his books change?","answer":"Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language. Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-060","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Formerly burdensome books become valued companions when he realises he may lose access to their language.","Imminent loss makes him recognise neglected educational opportunities."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?","answer":"They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-087","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives.","Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How does the poem treat appeals to stop the singing?","answer":"Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer. Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-100","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary responses fail to restrain the performer.","Repetition of distress and his persistence prepare for an unexpected physical interruption."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?","answer":"The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-098","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area.","Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-14","number":14,"title":"English Literature — Paper 14","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["walking at night","driving a racing car","repairing a television","robbing a bank"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","apparently purposeful intelligence","a chemical formula","a mechanical clock"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","ownership of a camera","a missing letter","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Titinius","Strato","Metellus Cimber","Caesar"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["painting portraits","playing the violin","running","writing a newspaper"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a lost crown","bribery","a forged will","a marriage proposal"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["Roman history","fractions","musical scales","participles"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["continuity of past lives in familiar places","the invention of a machine","a detective’s murder investigation","a war at sea"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","a forestry instruction manual","only a change in weather","a comic musical performance"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators","self-respect over a vain demand","fear preventing any action"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a forest expedition","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Alexandria","Athens","Sardinia","Philippi"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["Martin’s father","a photographer","a goat","a royal judge"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes","a race already won","traditional expectations of a woman’s body"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Portia","Strato","Pindarus","Brutus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Pindarus","Octavius","Cinna the poet","Lucius"],"correct":0}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"What is ironic about the institution to which Mead is taken?","answer":"Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction. The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-054","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction.","The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?","answer":"Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-053","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens.","Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the contrast between Mead’s home and the others matter near the end?","answer":"His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life. He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-055","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life.","He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the finished photograph fail even if its features are considered attractive?","answer":"Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion. It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-030","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Attractiveness is not the narrator’s main criterion.","It fails to preserve his individuality and therefore cannot serve the personal and emotional purpose for which he wanted it."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does the narrator’s reaction at the end expose the limits of professional expertise?","answer":"He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him. The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-031","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him.","The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?","answer":"The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-027","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned.","Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does the writer’s control over the page create a moral choice?","answer":"He could destroy the creature easily while writing. Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-091","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["He could destroy the creature easily while writing.","Recognising signs of an independent mind makes restraint an ethical decision rather than a physical necessity."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Which aspect of the speck’s behaviour suggests intelligence to the speaker?","answer":"Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion. The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-090","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion.","The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the title an apparent contradiction?","answer":"A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-092","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance.","The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?","answer":"The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-067","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place.","Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How do the poem’s ghosts differ from frightening conventional ghosts?","answer":"They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions. The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-065","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions.","The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Why are domestic spaces important to the poem?","answer":"Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before. Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-066","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Doorways, stairs and rooms connect everyday life with people who occupied them before.","Familiar surroundings make the past seem close to the living."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-15","number":15,"title":"English Literature — Paper 15","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators","self-respect over a vain demand","fear preventing any action"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a war at sea","the invention of a machine","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a detective’s murder investigation"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Calpurnia","Pindarus","Antony"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["Roman history","participles","fractions","musical scales"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["all subjects","French","Latin everywhere","German"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Lucius","Cinna the poet","Octavius","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["automated","drawn by horses","a private taxi","driven by Mead"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a marriage proposal","a lost crown","a forged will","bribery"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Athens","Alexandria","Sardinia","Philippi"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Strato","Titinius","Caesar","Metellus Cimber"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["test of her lover’s devotion","proof of the king’s poverty","lesson in medicine","vote for a new king"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Portia","Pindarus","Strato","Brutus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["painting portraits","writing a newspaper","playing the violin","running"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a race already won","the cost of shoes","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a forest expedition","a sea voyage","a school election"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["Martin’s father","a photographer","a royal judge","a goat"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What unusual features of the classroom signal a change?","answer":"The usual noise is absent, M. Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back. These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-058","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["The usual noise is absent, M.","Hamel wears formal clothes and village elders sit at the back.","These details prepare Franz for the seriousness of the final lesson."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is Franz initially reluctant to go to school?","answer":"He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand. The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-057","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand.","The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.","answer":"Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-033","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him.","Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Explain how repeated meetings with the woman affect the reader.","answer":"Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern. Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-038","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern.","Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?","answer":"He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-034","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it.","The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What makes the singer’s confidence comic?","answer":"He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-097","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress.","The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why is the goat’s intervention an effective ending?","answer":"An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot. Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-101","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot.","Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why is the setting at a royal court important?","answer":"The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-073","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The spectators and king make the act a public display.","The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why does the lover retrieve the glove but reject the lady?","answer":"Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him. He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-076","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Retrieval demonstrates courage; rejection refuses a relationship in which vanity endangers him.","He distinguishes bravery from submission to an unreasonable test."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-16","number":16,"title":"English Literature — Paper 16","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","lesson in medicine","test of her lover’s devotion","vote for a new king"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["musical scales","participles","Roman history","fractions"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a medical examination described in detail","the cost of shoes","traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a race already won"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Metellus Cimber","Caesar","Titinius","Strato"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Lucius","Pindarus","Cinna the poet","Octavius"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a forest expedition","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Strato","Portia","Pindarus","Brutus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence","a mechanical clock"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Sardinia","Philippi","Athens","Alexandria"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who ends the singer’s performance in The Power of Music?","answer":"a goat","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-19","options":["Martin’s father","a royal judge","a photographer","a goat"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["German","French","all subjects","Latin everywhere"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Antony","Pindarus","Calpurnia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket","a missing letter","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["painting portraits","playing the violin","writing a newspaper","running"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["a private taxi","drawn by horses","driven by Mead","automated"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["walking at night","robbing a bank","driving a racing car","repairing a television"],"correct":0}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?","answer":"Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-002","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger.","His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"How does Antony use Caesar’s refusal of the crown to challenge Brutus’s argument?","answer":"Brutus claims dangerous ambition. Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge. It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-006","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus claims dangerous ambition.","Antony recalls Caesar refusing the crown three times, supplying a public action apparently inconsistent with that charge.","It helps move the crowd from assent to doubt."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why does the narrator insist on ownership of his face?","answer":"The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned. Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-027","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The insistence asserts personal identity and dignity against an expert who behaves as though the face can be redesigned.","Its earnestness in an absurd situation also produces humour."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does the narrator’s reaction at the end expose the limits of professional expertise?","answer":"He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him. The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-031","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him.","The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How do the photographer’s comments on the narrator’s face establish his attitude?","answer":"He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material. His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-026","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He inspects individual features as faults and treats the customer as raw material.","His professional certainty diminishes the narrator’s authority over his own appearance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does Adjoa’s running challenge Nana’s judgement?","answer":"The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance. Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-043","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["The legs dismissed as inadequate enable exceptional performance.","Actual achievement overturns an appearance-based assessment and provides a different measure of capability."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What is Nana’s objection to Adjoa’s legs?","answer":"She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-041","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["She judges them too thin and measures their value against traditional expectations of a woman’s body and future motherhood, rather than the child’s own abilities."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why does Adjoa find adult conversation difficult to understand?","answer":"Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her. Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-042","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Adults use assumptions and disagreements they do not fully explain to her.","Her child perspective reveals gaps between their confident claims and what she can observe."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How do the poem’s ghosts differ from frightening conventional ghosts?","answer":"They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions. The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-065","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["They are quiet presences associated with former inhabitants rather than violent apparitions.","The tone invites reflection on continuity and memory rather than terror."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the poem connect the material and spiritual worlds?","answer":"It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate. Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-069","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate.","Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?","answer":"It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-068","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives.","Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the shift in tone develop the poem’s argument?","answer":"The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility. The change enacts gradual recovery.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-086","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The early disturbance and diminished perception give way to a more affirmative recognition of existence and responsibility.","The change enacts gradual recovery."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the poem represent the immediate effect of bereavement on perception?","answer":"The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception. Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur. This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities. The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-083","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception.","Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur.","This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities.","The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-17","number":17,"title":"English Literature — Paper 17","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["lesson in medicine","vote for a new king","proof of the king’s poverty","test of her lover’s devotion"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Octavius","Pindarus","Lucius","Cinna the poet"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Portia","Brutus","Strato","Pindarus"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a marriage proposal","a forged will","bribery","a lost crown"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Strato","Caesar","Titinius","Metellus Cimber"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["a missing letter","ownership of a camera","the price of a train ticket","likeness versus imposed improvement"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["robbing a bank","repairing a television","driving a racing car","walking at night"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["French","Latin everywhere","all subjects","German"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["fractions","participles","Roman history","musical scales"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a forest expedition","a sea voyage","a school election"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Philippi","Sardinia","Athens","Alexandria"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a race already won","the cost of shoes","traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","a forestry instruction manual","a comic musical performance","only a change in weather"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["self-respect over a vain demand","ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators","fear preventing any action"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["drawn by horses","a private taxi","driven by Mead","automated"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a chemical formula","a mechanical clock","a printed correction","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why is Brutus angry about Lucius Pella’s case?","answer":"Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation. He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar. Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-011","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation.","He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar.","Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Antony delay reading Caesar’s will?","answer":"Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it. The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar. Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-007","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Delay arouses curiosity and makes the crowd demand it.","The prospect of benefits establishes a personal connection with Caesar.","Antony directs the crowd while presenting himself as reluctant to inflame it."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does Martin’s father respond to his fear?","answer":"He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it. The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-034","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["He dismisses it as weakness and expects him to overcome it.","The response makes Martin ashamed and leaves him unable to seek reassuring understanding."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Explain how repeated meetings with the woman affect the reader.","answer":"Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern. Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-038","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Each recurrence prevents the fear from disappearing and gives it an apparently inescapable pattern.","Limited knowledge of the woman’s intentions sustains uncertainty."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"What effect is produced by the woman’s final greeting to Martin?","answer":"Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear. The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-039","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Her knowledge of him and the private encounter intensify his fear.","The ending stops without explaining her intentions or supplying a rescue, leaving the threat unresolved."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?","answer":"Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-053","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens.","Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the absence of other pedestrians matter?","answer":"It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional. It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-049","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["It makes Mead’s ordinary walk appear exceptional.","It also shows a society whose people withdraw into houses and screen entertainment, leaving public space empty."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How does the automated police car represent social authority?","answer":"Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories. Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-052","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Its impersonal questioning reduces Mead to a set of expected categories.","Its lack of a human occupant emphasises mechanical control without sympathetic judgement."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How does the final action change the meaning of the heroic rescue?","answer":"The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused. The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-079","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The danger is overcome, but the emotional reward is refused.","The act affirms self-respect rather than the lady’s power to demand sacrifice."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the king’s response contribute to the ending?","answer":"It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity. Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-077","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["It endorses the rejection and exposes the lady’s vanity.","Public judgement reverses the admiration she hoped the stunt would win."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the poem more than an observation of an insect?","answer":"The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being. The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-094","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The encounter prompts reflection on judgement, intelligence and the treatment of a weaker being.","The creature’s size tests the speaker’s willingness to recognise value."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"What does the decision to spare the speck reveal?","answer":"It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power. The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-095","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power.","The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the speck first easy to dismiss?","answer":"Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page. Close observation challenges that first judgement.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-089","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its tiny size makes it appear insignificant and it is initially seen against the writer’s working page.","Close observation challenges that first judgement."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the title an apparent contradiction?","answer":"A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-092","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance.","The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-18","number":18,"title":"English Literature — Paper 18","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["test of her lover’s devotion","lesson in medicine","vote for a new king","proof of the king’s poverty"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["a race already won","the cost of shoes","traditional expectations of a woman’s body","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["fear preventing any action","self-respect over a vain demand","hatred of all spectators","ignorance of danger"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The official order in The Last Lesson ends the teaching of","answer":"French","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-13","options":["all subjects","German","Latin everywhere","French"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["continuity of past lives in familiar places","the invention of a machine","a war at sea","a detective’s murder investigation"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["musical scales","participles","fractions","Roman history"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Portia","Pindarus","Strato","Brutus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a mechanical clock","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Antony","Pindarus","Calpurnia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a sea voyage","a school election","a forest expedition","the elevator and its disturbing encounters"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Cinna the poet","Octavius","Lucius","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Alexandria","Sardinia","Athens","Philippi"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a forged will","a marriage proposal","bribery","a lost crown"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["influential lives whose loss affects others","a forestry instruction manual","a comic musical performance","only a change in weather"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The photographer and narrator disagree chiefly about","answer":"likeness versus imposed improvement","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-6","options":["the price of a train ticket","ownership of a camera","likeness versus imposed improvement","a missing letter"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Metellus Cimber","Caesar","Titinius","Strato"],"correct":3}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Lucilius pretend to be Brutus, and how does Antony respond?","answer":"Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus. Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-022","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Lucilius risks capture to protect Brutus.","Antony recognises the loyalty behind the deception and orders him treated well, judging him a valuable honourable prisoner rather than punishing him immediately."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain the error that leads Cassius to order Pindarus to kill him.","answer":"Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture. Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost. In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-020","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Pindarus mistakes the soldiers surrounding Titinius and their shouts for capture.","Cassius concludes that his friend has been taken and his position is lost.","In fact the troops are friendly and celebrate Titinius."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Titinius place a garland on Cassius and then kill himself?","answer":"The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise. Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-021","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["The garland signifies the success and honour Cassius failed to recognise.","Finding Cassius dead because of a mistake, Titinius expresses loyalty and grief and uses Cassius’s sword to take his own life."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How is the quarrel between Brutus and Cassius resolved?","answer":"Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt; Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation. Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-013","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius offers Brutus his dagger and exposes his hurt;","Brutus softens, acknowledges anger and accepts reconciliation.","Their personal affection temporarily overcomes accusations and political differences."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Explain the disagreement over money sent to Brutus’s army.","answer":"Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably. Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger. Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-012","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus says Cassius denied funds when Brutus would not raise them dishonourably.","Cassius denies deliberately refusing him and blames the messenger.","Distrust turns a logistical dispute into a personal quarrel."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why is Brutus angry about Lucius Pella’s case?","answer":"Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation. He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar. Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-011","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Brutus condemns Pella for taking bribes despite Cassius’s recommendation.","He sees corrupt conduct as a betrayal of the honourable cause offered for killing Caesar.","Cassius considers the practical pressures of their situation."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Girl Who Can\nContext summary: Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"What does Nana’s response to Adjoa’s achievement reveal?","answer":"Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism. She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-045","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Pride and emotional involvement qualify her earlier criticism.","She can respond to evidence and affection even while representing traditional assumptions."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Explain the significance of the title in relation to Adjoa.","answer":"It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume. The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-047","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It affirms demonstrated ability rather than the limitations others assume.","The child becomes someone who can achieve, despite judgements imposed on her body."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"How does the first-person child narrator expose prejudice without a long direct argument?","answer":"Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty. Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-046","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Readers compare Adjoa’s puzzled observations with the adults’ certainty.","Her straightforward account allows the inconsistency of judging ability by appearance to emerge."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Why is the school setting important to Adjoa’s development?","answer":"It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance. The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-044","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["It creates an opportunity for her ability to be recognised through participation and performance.","The opportunity extends beyond the domestic expectations surrounding her."]},{"topic":"The Girl Who Can","prompt":"Compare Nana’s initial assessment with the outcome, and explain the story’s treatment of change.","answer":"Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations. Running supplies counter-evidence. Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-048","section":"Prose","family":"The Girl Who Can","context":"Adjoa hears adults debate her thin legs; her success in running challenges Nana’s judgement.","solutionSteps":["Initially Nana assesses Adjoa through conventional physical expectations.","Running supplies counter-evidence.","Nana’s pride suggests attitudes can be challenged through experience, while Adjoa gains recognition on the basis of capability."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is M. Hamel unable to complete his final spoken farewell?","answer":"Emotion overwhelms him. He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-063","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Emotion overwhelms him.","He turns to the blackboard and writes a patriotic declaration, making the silent action carry what he cannot say aloud."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What does Franz discover about the official order?","answer":"Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine. The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M. Hamel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-059","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine.","The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M.","Hamel."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"When Great Trees Fall\nContext summary: The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"How does the poem represent the immediate effect of bereavement on perception?","answer":"The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception. Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur. This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities. The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-083","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["The death changes the survivors’ experience of their surroundings: even breathing and seeing become strained, conveying how thoroughly grief disturbs ordinary perception.","Memory then becomes painfully sharp, dwelling on kind words left unsaid and shared experiences that will never occur.","This combines a diminished present with a heightened awareness of missed possibilities.","The loss is therefore intellectual and emotional as well as physical; the person who once helped give the world meaning is absent."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Why does the poem’s movement towards renewal not cancel grief?","answer":"Grief remains the starting reality. Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-085","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["Grief remains the starting reality.","Renewal grows from recognising the continuing possibilities inspired by the person, rather than pretending that the loss did not occur."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What responsibility do the living acquire through remembering great souls?","answer":"They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives. Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-087","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["They can live more fully and recognise capacities awakened by those lives.","Continuing influence becomes a reason to act rather than remain permanently diminished."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"What does the image of a great tree represent in the poem?","answer":"It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength. The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-081","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["It represents a person whose life gives others shelter, direction or strength.","The scale of the tree suggests the breadth of the loss caused by such a death."]},{"topic":"When Great Trees Fall","prompt":"Trace the movement from the tree image to the closing affirmation.","answer":"A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death. Human grief then affects perception and memory. The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-088","section":"Poetry","family":"When Great Trees Fall","context":"The poem compares the loss of influential people with the fall of great trees, then considers how life responds.","solutionSteps":["A large physical loss models the disruption of an influential death.","Human grief then affects perception and memory.","The later recovery recognises that the person’s existence enlarges the possibilities of those who remain, allowing affirmation without erasing sorrow."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What is ironic about the title?","answer":"Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony. Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-102","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony.","Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why are the reactions of animals and people presented together?","answer":"They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste. The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-099","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["They make the suffering seem universal and extend the joke beyond an individual listener’s taste.","The whole environment becomes a witness against the singer."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Why is the goat’s intervention an effective ending?","answer":"An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot. Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-101","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["An animal accomplishes what the listeners cannot.","Its direct physical action abruptly ends the inflated musical ordeal and reverses the performer’s dominance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-19","number":19,"title":"English Literature — Paper 19","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Pindarus","Brutus","Portia","Strato"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Cinna the poet","Octavius","Lucius","Pindarus"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a forestry instruction manual","influential lives whose loss affects others","only a change in weather","a comic musical performance"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Calpurnia","Pindarus","Portia","Antony"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Caesar’s ghost promises another meeting at","answer":"Philippi","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-4","options":["Athens","Philippi","Alexandria","Sardinia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["fear preventing any action","self-respect over a vain demand","ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["proof of the king’s poverty","vote for a new king","lesson in medicine","test of her lover’s devotion"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["drawn by horses","automated","driven by Mead","a private taxi"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["musical scales","participles","fractions","Roman history"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a forest expedition","a school election","a sea voyage","the elevator and its disturbing encounters"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["the cost of shoes","a medical examination described in detail","a race already won","traditional expectations of a woman’s body"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a mechanical clock","a chemical formula","apparently purposeful intelligence"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["a marriage proposal","a lost crown","a forged will","bribery"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["driving a racing car","robbing a bank","walking at night","repairing a television"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Strato","Titinius","Metellus Cimber","Caesar"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["the invention of a machine","a detective’s murder investigation","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a war at sea"],"correct":2}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain why Brutus refuses to allow the conspirators to kill Antony as well. What does this reveal about his political judgement?","answer":"Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger. His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-002","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus wants an act against tyranny rather than indiscriminate slaughter; he underestimates Antony’s independent power and assumes that Caesar’s death removes the danger.","His ethical intentions accompany a serious strategic error."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act V\nContext summary: The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why does Cassius send Titinius towards the approaching troops?","answer":"Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies. He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-019","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Cassius needs to know whether the troops are friends or enemies.","He sends Titinius to investigate and relies on Pindarus’s distant observations instead of obtaining a reliable direct report."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"How does Strato assist Brutus at the end?","answer":"After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it. This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-023","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["After other companions refuse to kill him, Brutus asks Strato to hold the sword and runs upon it.","This preserves Brutus’s sense of choosing his own end after military defeat."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"What disagreement occurs between Antony and Octavius about their positions in battle?","answer":"Antony directs Octavius to the left; Octavius chooses the right. Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-018","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony directs Octavius to the left;","Octavius chooses the right.","Octavius’s refusal to follow the instruction reveals independence within their alliance rather than complete submission to Antony."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Why is Cassius disturbed by the birds accompanying the army?","answer":"He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign. His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-017","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["He contrasts the earlier eagles with ravens, crows and kites and interprets the change as an ominous sign.","His admission that he now partly credits omens shows anxiety before battle."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act V","prompt":"Explain Antony’s final judgement of Brutus and its qualification of the play’s political conflict.","answer":"Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators. He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-024","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act V","context":"The armies meet at Philippi; mistaken reports lead to Cassius’s death, followed later by Brutus’s defeat.","solutionSteps":["Antony distinguishes Brutus’s concern for the public good from the envy motivating other conspirators.","He praises Brutus’s balanced nature and honour, acknowledging moral worth in a defeated opponent while not undoing the disastrous consequences of his act."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Pedestrian\nContext summary: Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why is walking simply to observe considered abnormal in this society?","answer":"Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens. Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-053","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Leisure is expected to occur indoors with screens.","Mead’s independent curiosity and unstructured movement do not fit the system’s prescribed behaviour."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"How do descriptions of the houses contribute to the atmosphere?","answer":"The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light. The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-050","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["The houses seem dark, silent and tomb-like apart from screen light.","The imagery suggests isolation and diminished public life rather than a lively neighbourhood."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"What is ironic about the institution to which Mead is taken?","answer":"Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction. The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-054","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Ordinary independent behaviour is classified as a condition requiring correction.","The society’s idea of normality is itself disturbing to the reader."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Why does the contrast between Mead’s home and the others matter near the end?","answer":"His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life. He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-055","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["His brightly lit home contrasts with the surrounding darkness and symbolises a more active individual life.","He is removed from that life by the conformity-enforcing system."]},{"topic":"The Pedestrian","prompt":"Explain how setting and the encounter develop the conflict between individuality and conformity.","answer":"Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city. The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference. His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-056","section":"Prose","family":"The Pedestrian","context":"Leonard Mead walks through a screen-dominated city and is questioned by an automated police car.","solutionSteps":["Mead’s attentive walking contrasts with the empty screen-bound city.","The police questions deny the legitimacy of harmless difference.","His detention turns social sameness into coercion rather than a free preference."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"With the Photographer\nContext summary: A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Explain the irony in the photographer’s confidence about retouching.","answer":"He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements. For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness. Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-028","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He regards increasingly drastic alterations as achievements.","For the narrator, each improvement makes the picture less useful as a likeness.","Technical success becomes failure in the customer’s terms."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Why is the narrator’s purpose in visiting the studio important to the conflict?","answer":"He wants a recognisable likeness of himself. The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important. Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-025","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He wants a recognisable likeness of himself.","The photographer assumes that technically improving the face is more important.","Their incompatible definitions of a satisfactory photograph create the conflict."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does first-person narration contribute to the humour?","answer":"Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly. His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-029","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["Readers follow the customer’s increasing discomfort and indignation directly.","His restrained account of absurd professional behaviour exposes the photographer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"How does the narrator’s reaction at the end expose the limits of professional expertise?","answer":"He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him. The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-031","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["He rejects the altered portrait because it does not represent him.","The expert’s ability to manipulate an image does not authorise him to replace the subject’s wishes."]},{"topic":"With the Photographer","prompt":"Discuss the conflict between appearance and identity using the visit, the alterations and the ending.","answer":"The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness. Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal. The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-032","section":"Prose","family":"With the Photographer","context":"A customer asks for a photograph; the photographer treats his real features as defects to be altered.","solutionSteps":["The visit begins with a wish for a personal likeness.","Repeated criticism and retouching replace the individual with a supposed ideal.","The narrator’s rejection asserts that identity and recognisability matter more than imposed perfection."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"A Considerable Speck\nContext summary: A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"How does humour affect the speaker’s reflection?","answer":"The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful. It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-093","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["The disproportion between a writer and a tiny visitor makes the meditation playful.","It avoids pompous moralising while still taking perceived intelligence seriously."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Which aspect of the speck’s behaviour suggests intelligence to the speaker?","answer":"Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion. The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-090","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["Its apparently purposeful movement, hesitation and response to danger seem different from mechanical motion.","The inference comes from observed behaviour rather than size."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"What does the decision to spare the speck reveal?","answer":"It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power. The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-095","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["It reveals respect for a perceived independent mind and the deliberate restraint of power.","The speaker values mental life without requiring physical grandeur."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Why is the title an apparent contradiction?","answer":"A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance. The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-092","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["A speck is minute, whereas considerable suggests importance.","The poem separates physical smallness from the significance of thought or life."]},{"topic":"A Considerable Speck","prompt":"Explain how observation changes the speaker’s response and supports the title.","answer":"What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour. The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it. The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-096","section":"Poetry","family":"A Considerable Speck","context":"A writer notices a minute moving creature on his manuscript and observes it before deciding its fate.","solutionSteps":["What initially seems negligible displays purposeful behaviour.","The speaker revises his judgement and chooses to spare it.","The creature becomes considerable through the intelligence he perceives, giving the title its comic and moral force."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"Haunted Houses\nContext summary: The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the poem connect the material and spiritual worlds?","answer":"It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate. Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-069","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It presents them as overlapping rather than completely separate.","Images of passage and connection allow the unseen to influence ordinary experience."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"How does the speaker question exclusive ownership of a house?","answer":"The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place. Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-067","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The living possess legal title, but the lives and memories of earlier occupants also belong to the place.","Ownership is set against historical and emotional continuity."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What does the title suggest once the whole poem is considered?","answer":"Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives. The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-071","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["Being haunted means retaining traces of earlier lives.","The title broadens from a ghost-story expectation into a meditation on memory and spiritual presence."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"What effect does the image of invisible guests produce?","answer":"It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives. Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-068","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["It suggests that the present contains more lives and memories than the eye perceives.","Ordinary social activity becomes a meeting of visible and invisible company."]},{"topic":"Haunted Houses","prompt":"Explain how two images develop the continuing relationship between past and present.","answer":"The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life. These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there. The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space. Both images make past and present coexist. The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-072","section":"Poetry","family":"Haunted Houses","context":"The speaker imagines past inhabitants continuing to share the places occupied by living people.","solutionSteps":["The quiet movement of former inhabitants through familiar rooms makes the past part of ordinary domestic life.","These presences do not attack the living; they suggest that a place retains the lives once lived there.","The image of unseen guests extends the same idea into a social gathering: visible people are not its only company, because memory and earlier lives also occupy the space.","Both images make past and present coexist.","The calm, reflective tone turns haunting into continuity rather than a conventional supernatural threat."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]},{"id":"english2-paper-20","number":20,"title":"English Literature — Paper 20","marks":80,"time":"2 hours","sections":[{"title":"Section A — compulsory","questions":[{"label":"1","prompt":"Choose the correct answers.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"In The Glove and the Lions, the lady seeks a public","answer":"test of her lover’s devotion","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-15","options":["test of her lover’s devotion","lesson in medicine","proof of the king’s poverty","vote for a new king"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Franz’s unprepared school exercise concerns","answer":"participles","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-12","options":["Roman history","musical scales","participles","fractions"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The speck’s movement makes the speaker recognise","answer":"apparently purposeful intelligence","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-18","options":["a printed correction","a mechanical clock","apparently purposeful intelligence","a chemical formula"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Adjoa’s achievement is in","answer":"running","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-8","options":["running","playing the violin","painting portraits","writing a newspaper"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Which character defends Brutus’s honour after his death?","answer":"Antony","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-5","options":["Portia","Pindarus","Calpurnia","Antony"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who permits Antony to speak at Caesar’s funeral under conditions?","answer":"Brutus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-0","options":["Pindarus","Brutus","Strato","Portia"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The police vehicle in The Pedestrian is","answer":"automated","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-11","options":["automated","drawn by horses","a private taxi","driven by Mead"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who interprets Titinius’s reception by soldiers incorrectly?","answer":"Pindarus","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-1","options":["Octavius","Lucius","Pindarus","Cinna the poet"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The great trees metaphorically suggest","answer":"influential lives whose loss affects others","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-17","options":["a forestry instruction manual","influential lives whose loss affects others","only a change in weather","a comic musical performance"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The lover’s final rejection chiefly asserts","answer":"self-respect over a vain demand","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-16","options":["fear preventing any action","ignorance of danger","hatred of all spectators","self-respect over a vain demand"],"correct":3},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Who holds the sword for Brutus at the end?","answer":"Strato","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-2","options":["Titinius","Strato","Caesar","Metellus Cimber"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Nana initially judges Adjoa’s legs by","answer":"traditional expectations of a woman’s body","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-9","options":["traditional expectations of a woman’s body","the cost of shoes","a race already won","a medical examination described in detail"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The title Haunted Houses chiefly develops the idea of","answer":"continuity of past lives in familiar places","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-14","options":["a war at sea","continuity of past lives in familiar places","a detective’s murder investigation","the invention of a machine"],"correct":1},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Leonard Mead is detained while","answer":"walking at night","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-10","options":["driving a racing car","robbing a bank","walking at night","repairing a television"],"correct":2},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"The dispute over Lucius Pella chiefly concerns","answer":"bribery","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-3","options":["bribery","a lost crown","a forged will","a marriage proposal"],"correct":0},{"topic":"Compulsory MCQ","prompt":"Martin’s central fear concerns","answer":"the elevator and its disturbing encounters","marks":1,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-mcq-7","options":["a school election","a forest expedition","the elevator and its disturbing encounters","a sea voyage"],"correct":2}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer all questions."},{"title":"Section B — Drama","questions":[{"label":"2","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act IV\nContext summary: The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does Brutus disclose about Portia, and why is the disclosure significant here?","answer":"Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation. Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-014","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Portia has died by swallowing fire, distressed by Brutus’s absence and the political situation.","Brutus’s controlled disclosure explains part of his emotional strain and shows the personal cost of the conflict."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Why does Antony compare Lepidus to an animal used to carry burdens?","answer":"He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker. The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-010","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["He regards Lepidus as useful for tasks rather than as an equal political judgement-maker.","The comparison shows contempt and Antony’s wish to control the partnership."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"Give Cassius’s reason for waiting for the enemy and Brutus’s reason for marching to Philippi.","answer":"Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests. Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-015","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["Cassius wants the enemy to tire and consume resources while their army rests.","Brutus fears the enemy will gather support from the people between them and argues that they should act at a favourable moment."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"What does the proscription scene reveal about Antony and Octavius?","answer":"They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses. Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-009","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["They bargain over people marked for death, including relatives, and discuss using Caesar’s will to meet expenses.","Political calculation contrasts with the public image of loyalty surrounding Caesar’s funeral."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act IV","prompt":"How does Caesar’s ghost affect the close of Act IV?","answer":"The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi. Brutus questions it but cannot control it. It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-016","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act IV","context":"The triumvirs prepare their campaign. Brutus and Cassius quarrel, reconcile, and discuss marching to Philippi.","solutionSteps":["The apparition identifies itself as Brutus’s evil spirit and promises another meeting at Philippi.","Brutus questions it but cannot control it.","It connects Caesar’s continuing influence with the approaching battle and Brutus’s fate."]}]},{"label":"3","prompt":"Julius Caesar Act III\nContext summary: Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Brutus use the contrast between his love for Caesar and his love for Rome?","answer":"He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection. The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot. His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-004","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["He presents assassination as public duty overcoming personal affection.","The contrast asks citizens to judge him as a patriot.","His appeal depends on their accepting that Caesar threatened their freedom."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Why does Caesar reject Metellus Cimber’s petition, and what does his response reveal about his self-image?","answer":"Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother. Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed. This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-001","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Metellus seeks the recall of his banished brother.","Caesar refuses to be moved by pleading and presents his judgement as fixed.","This confidence in constancy makes his vulnerability to the conspirators ironic."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Explain how Antony’s repeated praise of the conspirators changes its effect during the funeral speech.","answer":"The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge. Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-005","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["The apparently respectful description becomes ironic as Antony supplies evidence against the ambition charge.","Repetition lets listeners reach the accusation themselves while Antony appears to obey the restriction on blaming the conspirators."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"State three conditions Brutus imposes on Antony’s funeral speech.","answer":"Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place. Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-003","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Antony must not blame the conspirators; he must acknowledge their permission; he must speak after Brutus at the same public place.","Credit equivalent precise accounts of the conditions."]},{"topic":"Julius Caesar Act III","prompt":"Compare Brutus’s and Antony’s methods of addressing the citizens.","answer":"Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom. Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion. Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-008","section":"Drama","family":"Julius Caesar Act III","context":"Caesar is killed at the Capitol; Brutus permits Antony to address the citizens under conditions.","solutionSteps":["Brutus offers a concise justification based on honour, reason and civic freedom.","Antony uses irony, concrete memories, wounds, the will and controlled emotion.","Both seek public judgement, but Antony adapts his speech to the crowd’s developing responses."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section C — Prose","questions":[{"label":"4","prompt":"The Last Lesson\nContext summary: Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"What does Franz discover about the official order?","answer":"Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine. The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M. Hamel.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-059","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Only German is to be taught in the schools of Alsace and Lorraine.","The lesson is therefore the last French lesson under M.","Hamel."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why is Franz initially reluctant to go to school?","answer":"He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand. The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-057","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He is unprepared for the lesson on participles and fears reprimand.","The pleasant outdoors is more attractive to him than the classroom duties he has neglected."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"How does M. Hamel distribute responsibility for neglected learning?","answer":"He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses. The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-062","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["He criticises pupils’ postponement, parents’ preference for work and his own past lapses.","The blame is shared rather than placed only on Franz."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Why have the elders come to the classroom?","answer":"They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service. Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-061","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["They regret neglecting education and wish to honour the teacher’s long service.","Their attendance expresses respect for their language and shared identity."]},{"topic":"The Last Lesson","prompt":"Trace Franz’s change and connect it to language and identity.","answer":"Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it. The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance. Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-064","section":"Prose","family":"The Last Lesson","context":"Franz arrives at school to learn that an official order has ended French teaching in Alsace and Lorraine.","solutionSteps":["Franz begins afraid of a school exercise and willing to avoid it.","The order makes him understand language as a threatened shared inheritance.","Attentive listening and regret replace indifference; the teacher’s final act gives that recognition public emotional force."]}]},{"label":"5","prompt":"The Elevator\nContext summary: Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is Martin’s injury important to the development of suspense?","answer":"It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder. The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-037","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["It limits his freedom of movement and makes escape or choosing the stairs harder.","The physical injury turns emotional fear into greater practical helplessness."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How does using the stairs fail to solve Martin’s problem?","answer":"The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him. Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-036","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The stairs are also difficult and frightening to him; an accident injures him.","Avoidance increases his vulnerability and eventually makes him more dependent on the elevator."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Give three features of the elevator that contribute to Martin’s fear.","answer":"Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him. Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-033","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["Its confined space, poor lighting and unreliable-seeming movement/closing mechanism make it threatening to him.","Explain how each feature increases claustrophobia rather than merely listing adjectives."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"Why is the woman’s physical presence particularly disturbing inside the elevator?","answer":"She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin. Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-035","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["She occupies much of the small space and seems to stare at Martin.","Her proximity combines his existing fear of confinement with uncertainty about her intentions."]},{"topic":"The Elevator","prompt":"How do setting, parental response and the open ending work together?","answer":"The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices. The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-040","section":"Prose","family":"The Elevator","context":"Martin fears the small elevator in his building; encounters with a large woman intensify his anxiety.","solutionSteps":["The confined elevator creates fear; the father’s dismissal isolates Martin; injury restricts his choices.","The unresolved final encounter leaves readers sharing uncertainty instead of receiving a complete explanation."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."},{"title":"Section D — Poetry","questions":[{"label":"6","prompt":"The Power of Music\nContext summary: Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What makes the singer’s confidence comic?","answer":"He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress. The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-097","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["He is absorbed in his performance while the surrounding world reacts with distress.","The mismatch between self-satisfaction and actual effect undermines his view of his talent."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"Explain the role of sound and rhythm in the comic narrative.","answer":"The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth. Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable. Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension. The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain. Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-103","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The lively rhythm carries the performance forward with a force out of proportion to its musical worth.","Repeated distress among listeners makes the song sound relentless rather than pleasurable.","Their appeals to stop contrast with the singer’s persistence, so each continuing disturbance increases the comic tension.","The goat’s abrupt physical intervention supplies a decisive change: action silences a voice that ordinary requests could not restrain.","Sound, narrative pace and the sudden ending together expose the singer’s self-importance."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do exaggerated geographical effects create humour?","answer":"The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area. Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-098","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["The voice appears to disturb an impossibly wide area.","Hyperbole magnifies an unpleasant performance into a fantastic public calamity."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"What is ironic about the title?","answer":"Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony. Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-102","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Music conventionally suggests pleasure or harmony.","Here its power is represented as disruptive and painful, making the title praise the performance in a comic opposite sense."]},{"topic":"The Power of Music","prompt":"How do hyperbole, contrast and the final reversal work together?","answer":"Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress. A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-104","section":"Poetry","family":"The Power of Music","context":"Bhishma Lochan Sharma sings with such exaggerated effects that people and animals suffer until a goat intervenes.","solutionSteps":["Impossible effects inflate the song into a catastrophe; the singer’s delight contrasts with widespread distress.","A goat’s simple action defeats the supposedly powerful performer, completing the satire of self-important bad singing."]}]},{"label":"7","prompt":"The Glove and the Lions\nContext summary: At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","marks":16,"parts":[{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Explain the irony of the lady’s plan.","answer":"She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige. The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-078","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She seeks a public proof of love that will enhance her prestige.","The successful retrieval instead becomes the occasion for public rejection."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"What does the lady expect her lover to prove?","answer":"She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-075","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["She expects him to retrieve the glove as evidence of devotion and to make her the admired centre of a spectacular romantic gesture."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Why is the setting at a royal court important?","answer":"The spectators and king make the act a public display. The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-073","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The spectators and king make the act a public display.","The lady’s demand is shaped by the possibility of gaining attention and admiration before an audience."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"How are the lions presented before the glove is dropped?","answer":"Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger. This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game.","marks":3,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-074","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["Their violent movement and power establish genuine physical danger.","This makes the test a serious misuse of courage rather than a harmless game."]},{"topic":"The Glove and the Lions","prompt":"Discuss the distinction between love and vanity in the lady’s test and its outcome.","answer":"The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety. The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection. The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display.","marks":4,"difficulty":"Standard","id":"english2-080","section":"Poetry","family":"The Glove and the Lions","context":"At a royal lion-fight a lady drops her glove among the animals to test her lover publicly.","solutionSteps":["The test values spectacle over the beloved’s safety.","The lover shows courage but refuses that condition of affection.","The king’s approval makes the closing judgement favour dignity and sincere care over theatrical display."]}]}],"attempt":1,"marks":16,"instructions":"Answer at least ONE question from this section. Across Sections B–D, answer FOUR questions in total. Each is 16 marks."}],"additionalChoiceMarks":16,"adaptation":"Close-reading mocks use original context summaries instead of reproducing copyrighted Treasure Chest extracts. Use the prescribed texts while checking literary evidence.","instructions":["Spend 15 minutes reading before starting the writing time.","Follow the choices printed in each section. Marks in brackets are the intended marks for this original practice paper.","Answer all of Section A. Answer FOUR questions from Sections B–D: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry, plus one additional question from any of these sections."]}],"plan":{"strategy":"Give extra practice to the recent long-question gaps, while keeping concise notes for every prescribed text.","core":[],"backup":[],"light":[],"rationale":"Original syllabus-scoped practice is arranged by skill and chapter. Large banks contain parameter and context variants of named question families, not thousands of distinct concepts.","source":"","sessions":[["Gap candidates","2 × 60 min","Prepare With the Photographer and Haunted Houses: context, tone, two precise images/events, technique and title. Write one 16-mark response on each. Their absence from 2025–26 long questions makes them coverage gaps, not guaranteed selections."],["Recent texts","3 × 50 min","Revise all other prose and poems using a one-page evidence sheet each. Retain 2026 selections; the 2027 specimen reuses 2025 long selections, so a last-year exclusion rule is unsupported."],["Julius Caesar","3 × 60 min","One session per Act III, IV and V. Trace events, speaker/addressee, motives, irony and consequences. Compare Brutus/Antony and Brutus/Cassius with precise moments, keeping scene chronology correct."],["Answer selection","2 hours + 30 min review","Attempt 16 MCQs and four literature answers: at least one Drama, one Prose and one Poetry. For each three/four-mark part write distinct supported points; review factual errors against your prescribed book."]]},"edition":"2026-11-16-worked"};
